Z||000004||DaRkLoRD||07-02-2000||01:50 AM||||what's a "stall converter"?<P>------------------<BR> <A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||olympic||07-02-2000||03:59 AM||||  Its a special torque converter that lets you rev the engine higher(while the tranny is in gear and your foot on the brakes) than the stock torque converter.  Its purpose is to allow you to get you engine rpm up into its power band for a good launch.  A stock converter usually wont let you raise the rpm's past 1200 or so while the car is motionless.  Obviously a high "stall" rpm would make street driving very difficult.  It also generates alot of heat because of the increased slippage.  I've seen race converters as high as 5000 rpm.  A good street/strip stall converter is usually rated at around 2000-2200 rpm.  This allows you to bring the rpm's up a little higher into the torque curve during launch  and still provides a low enough lockup rpm for highway cruising.<P>------------------<BR>"Born free....taxed to death."||||reg||1
